.TH CSHELL 1 "November 2026" "Operating Systems I" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cshell \- Custom Shell that combines the CSC332 Group 7 commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cshell
[\fB\-x\fR] [\fIscript\fR]
.PP
.B cshell
[\fB\-x\fR]
.B \-c
.I "command line"
.PP
.B cshell \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cshell
is a small command shell. It reads command lines from the terminal, from a
script file, or from
.BR \-c ,
and runs them.
.PP
The team commands are linked into
.B cshell
itself and are called directly:
.TP 18
.B iotracker
(Command 1) monitor a process's real-time I/O activity
.TP
.B filecrypt
(Command 3) encrypt or decrypt a file with AES-256-GCM
.TP
.B processgroup
(Command 4) launch, signal and monitor a group of commands
.TP
.B loganalyzer
(Command 5) count log levels and keywords in a log file
.TP
.B sharedmempipe
(Command 6) move data between processes through shared memory
.TP
.B filediffadvanced
(Command 8) compare two files byte by byte and line by line
.PP
Any other name, such as
.B ls
or
.BR cat ,
is run as a normal program found through
.BR PATH .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " line"
Run one command line and exit with its status.
.TP
.B \-x
Print each command (as
.BR "cshell> command" )
before running it. Useful for demo scripts.
.TP
.B \-h
Show usage information and exit.
.SH SYNTAX
.TP 18
.B cmd args...
Run a command.
.TP
.B \(dqa b\(dq  \(aqa b\(aq
Quotes keep spaces, |, < and > inside one argument. Inside double quotes,
\e\(dq and \e\e are escapes.
.TP
.B cmd < file
Read standard input from
.IR file .
.TP
.B cmd > file
Write standard output to
.IR file ,
replacing it.
.TP
.B cmd >> file
Append standard output to
.IR file .
.TP
.B cmd1 | cmd2
Connect the output of
.I cmd1
to the input of
.IR cmd2 .
Up to 16 stages.
.TP
.B # text
A comment; the rest of the line is ignored.
.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS
.TP 18
.B help [command]
List all commands, or show the usage of one team command.
.TP
.B cd [dir]
Change directory (default:
.BR $HOME ).
.TP
.B exit [status]
Leave the shell. Ctrl+D at the prompt also exits.
.SH SIGNALS
Ctrl+C stops the running command, not the shell. At an empty prompt it
just shows a new prompt. Each command runs in its own child process with
the default signal settings, so a command's own handlers (for example
iotracker printing its summary) still work.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of the last command run, 128 + N if it was killed by
signal N, 127 if the command was not found, 2 for a syntax error, or the
value given to
.BR exit .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cshell:project> help
cshell:project> iotracker -i 0.5 \-\- dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/x bs=1M count=100
cshell:project> filecrypt \-e \-p secret notes.txt notes.enc
cshell:project> processgroup \-i 1 \-\- sleep 2 ::: sleep 3
cshell:project> loganalyzer \-k ERROR \-v app.log > errors.txt
cshell:project> cat big.bin | sharedmempipe > copy.bin
cshell:project> filediffadvanced \-t a.txt b.txt
.fi
.PP
Run a prepared demo, echoing each command:
.PP
.RS
.B cshell \-x demo.txt
.RE
.SH IMPLEMENTATION
Each team command's source file is compiled with
.BI \- NAME _NO_MAIN
so that only its
.IB name _main()
function is linked in. For every command line,
.B cshell
creates the pipes with
.BR pipe (2),
starts one child per stage with
.BR fork (2),
connects input and output with
.BR dup2 (2),
then either calls the team command's function or uses
.BR execvp (3).
The shell waits for all stages with
.BR waitpid (2).
.BR sigaction (2)
keeps the shell alive on SIGINT and ignores SIGQUIT.
.SH LIMITATIONS
No variables, wildcards (*), command separators (; && ||), background
jobs (&) or job control. Commands that run other commands (processgroup,
iotracker) read their own options first, so put
.B \-\-
before a command that has options of its own, e.g.
.BR "processgroup \-\- sh \-c \(dqecho hi\(dq" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iotracker (1),
.BR filecrypt (1),
.BR processgroup (1),
.BR loganalyzer (1),
.BR sharedmempipe (1),
.BR filediffadvanced (1),
.BR sh (1)
